<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sophia Palaiologina</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1455–<a href="/years/1503">1503</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Zoe Palaiologina · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palaiologos-dynasty">Palaiologos dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sophia Palaiologina was a Byzantine princess, niece of the last emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine XI</a>, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1472. Brought up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, she came to Moscow through papal mediation, though hopes of drawing Moscow into union with Rome came to nothing. The marriage strengthened Muscovite claims to the Byzantine heritage, and her son became grand prince as Vasily III.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sophia-palaiologina.jpg" alt="Sophia Palaiologina" width="320" height="532" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sergey Nikitin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S.paleolog_reconstruction03.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sophia, born Zoe Palaiologina, was the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-palaiologos">Thomas Palaiologos</a>, Despot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> and younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine XI</a>, and of Catherine, heiress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/centurione-ii-zaccaria">Centurione II Zaccaria</a>, the last independent Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>. After the Ottoman army breached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hexamilion-wall">Hexamilion wall</a> in 1460, the family fled to Corfu and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Her mother died on Corfu in 1462, and when her dying father recalled the children to Rome in 1465 they were adopted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">Papacy</a> and lived at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a>. Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarion">Basilios Bessarion</a>, a Greek humanist and scholar, had charge of the children. They received 3,600 crowns for clothes, horses and servants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Maria of Tver, the first wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a>, died in 1467, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-ii">Pope Paul II</a> proposed Sophia as his bride in 1469, probably hoping to extend Catholic influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and unite the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and Catholic churches. Ivan Fryazin, acting as proxy for the grand prince, married her at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-peters-basilica">St. Peter's Basilica</a> on 1 June 1472. She reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 12 November 1472, when the wedding was held at the Dormition Cathedral. The papal plan failed at once: she returned to the faith of her fathers after the wedding, and the papal legate was not allowed to enter Moscow carrying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> cross.</p><p>She did not keep to the isolation of elite Russian women and received foreign envoys, among them the Venetian Ambrogio Contarini in 1476. She is believed to have introduced grand Byzantine ceremony to the Kremlin and to have urged Ivan to end his tributary relationship with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>. She bore eleven children between 1474 and 1490, among them the future Vasili III.</p><p>The court divided between supporters of the heir Ivan the Young, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a>'s son by his first wife, and Sophia's party. When Ivan the Young died in 1490, rumour said that she had poisoned him, but modern historians say the claim cannot be verified for lack of sources.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1498 Ivan the Young's son Dmitry was crowned co-ruler, while Sophia and Vasili were left out, though they returned to favour in 1499. In April 1502 the struggle ended: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> placed Dmitry and his mother Elena under guard, and Vasili was crowned grand prince. Sophia died on 7 April 1503. She was buried in the Ascension Convent in the Kremlin, and when the convent was demolished in 1929 her remains were moved to the Cathedral of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkhangelsk">Archangel</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sophia Palaiologina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ivan's marriage to <b>Sophia Palaiologina</b> in 1472, his use of the double-headed eagle and the title 'sovereign of all Rus'' expressed a growing claim to autocratic, quasi-imperial authority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Ivan III had married <b>Sophia Palaiologina</b>, niece of the last Byzantine emperor, used the double-headed eagle and occasionally styled himself tsar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mystras</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.07,22.37&amp;year=1486" aria-label="Mystras on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5110 828 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sophia Palaiologina"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5560 1098h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5560 1098h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.07,22.37&amp;year=1486">What was here in 1486? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
